<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
        <string>Klandagi</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Klandagi uses the camera only to scan a position QR code shown on another phone, so you can share locations with no signal. Nothing is recorded, stored, or sent anywhere.</string>
	<!-- These must describe what the app actually does. The first string used to
	     say "Location never leaves the device except in the emergency messages
	     you send yourself", which is contradicted by flushActivityPoints in
	     src/lib/offline/index.ts: it POSTs batches of {lat, lng, elevation,
	     recordedAt} to this app's own backend so a finished hike survives losing
	     the phone. -->
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Klandagi shows your position on the downloaded trail map and works out off-route alerts on this phone, with no signal. When you are back in range, finished tracks are uploaded to Klandagi's own server so they survive losing this phone. Nothing is sold or shared, and there is no advertising or analytics.</string>
	<key>NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>While you are navigating or recording, Klandagi keeps your position updating with the screen off or the phone in a pocket — otherwise the breadcrumb you would follow home, your off-route alerts, and your check-in timer all stop without telling you. This runs only while a hike is open, and finished tracks are uploaded to Klandagi's own server when you are back in range.</string>
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>location</string>
	</array>
	<key>UILaunchStoryboardName</key>
	<string>LaunchScreen</string>
	<key>UIMainStoryboardFile</key>
	<string>Main</string>
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<string>armv7</string>
	</array>
	<!-- Portrait only, matching public/manifest.json and the web compass path,
	     which refuses a device-orientation sample unless the screen orientation
	     is exactly 0. The port widened this to landscape without widening any of
	     the code written against it: the heading was uncorrected for device
	     orientation, and the navigate screen's alert layer is clipped to about
	     52px by a max-height:480px media query. Re-enable landscape only
	     together with those. -->
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
